#heading(level: 2, numbering: none)[Abstract]

Nearly 70% of digital transformation initiatives fail due to flawed technological decisions in the pre-project phase and subsequent technical debt accumulation. While frameworks like SWEBOK formalize engineering decision-making, organizations systematically bypass this analysis due to the prohibitive time and cost of manually eliciting intangible assets. To eliminate this bottleneck, we present _AI-Driven DTGEP_, a methodology that integrates conversational AI agents into pre-project decision-making. Adapting the DTGEP framework into an asynchronous model, an autonomous agent elicits qualitative data and structures it for deterministic algorithmic execution: maturity assessment (ALTUS), intangible asset and solution deduction (SIPAC), and multicriteria prioritization (AHP). Empirical evaluation across four real-world organizations examined seven language models (from local 4B architectures on consumer hardware to cloud frontier models) alongside a direct-prompting baseline. Results show that agents reliably assess digital maturity with a Mean Absolute Error (MAE) of 1.06–1.27 levels (matching human inter-assessor variability), achieve 88.9% Weighted Recall in identifying candidate technological solutions, and guarantee strict mathematical consistency in prioritization ($"CR" < 0.10$, ratios 0.04–0.08). Compared to direct prompting, which hallucinates and violates formal consistency, AI-Driven DTGEP demonstrates that AI can streamline pre-project consulting while preserving strict engineering rigor.

*Keywords:* Agentic Artificial Intelligence, Engineering Decision-Making, Requirements Elicitation, Software Pre-Project, DTGEP.
